  {
   "id": "yF-inEVUEAAJ",
   "from": "Finn Lindgren",
   "author": "afb003e064114795",
   "date": "2019-01-04T19:57:36Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Change of support for log-Gaussian Cox process model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"auto\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>Yes, in principle this type of model should be possible with inlabru. The difficulty lies in being able to express the predictor/likelihood for the areal observations. We have been thinking of how to best allow the user to express this in the interface; a SpatialPolygonsDataFrame with the counts for each polygon seems the obvious way, but we haven’t implemented this yet.</div><div><br></div><div> It is almost certainly already possible to explicitly construct the required expression as a generic bru() model, since one “only” needs to express the log-integral</div><div>\\log \\int Lambda(s) ds,</div><div>for each polygon, which would feed directly into a regular Poisson count likelihood. The integration scheme can use the same ipoints() construction as the lgcp() models use. I just haven’t had time to test that properly, as we’ve been workin in getting the internal backend code updated so we can support the full range of latent models. I recall running into some complication for how to write the code in a general way, likely relating to the way inlabru indata is handled (it will be easier to handle as a special likelihood within inlabru than by the user in the predictor). I’m happy to discuss this further off-list.<br><br><div dir=\"ltr\">Finn</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"auto\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><br>On 4 Jan 2019, at 19:39, John Best &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jkb...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
